[?25l[0m[38;5;141m> [0m[38;5;252m[1m## What was wrong and what I changed[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The bug was a [1munits mismatch[22m in calls to [38;5;10mblake512_update[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The C BLAKE-512 implementation has a quirky API: [38;5;10mblake512_update(S, data, datalen)[0m takes [38;5;10mdatalen[0m in [1mbits[22m, not bytes. The top-level convenience function [38;5;10mblake512(out, in, inlen)[0m takes [38;5;10minlen[0m in [1mbytes[22m and internally multiplies by 8 before calling [38;5;10mblake512_update[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
However, the C code in [38;5;10mhash_blake.c[0m (for [38;5;10mgen_message_random[0m and [38;5;10mhash_message[0m) and [38;5;10mPQCgenKAT_sign.c[0m (for the KAT transcript) calls [38;5;10mblake512_update[0m directly, passing [1mbyte counts[22m — e.g., [38;5;10mblakeX_update(&S, sk_prf, SPX_N)[0m where [38;5;10mSPX_N = 32[0m. This means it actually hashes only 32 bits (4 bytes) of data, not 32 bytes. This is the intended behavior of the original C code.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The Rust translation "helpfully" multiplied all these values by 8, thinking it was converting bytes to bits to match the function's documented interface. But this changed the semantics — hashing 256 bits (32 bytes) instead of 32 bits (4 bytes), producing completely different output.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
